{
  // wrangler config for the OpenNext Cloudflare build.
  //
  // `main` and `assets.directory` point at OpenNext's build output (created by
  // `npx opennextjs-cloudflare build`). The `nodejs_compat` flag is REQUIRED —
  // the app uses node:crypto (admin auth, club tokens) and Buffer.
  //
  // Secrets (STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET, STRIPE_BREAD_CLUB_PRICE_ID,
  // SANITY_API_WRITE_TOKEN, CLUB_LINK_SECRET, BAKER_TOKEN, RESEND_API_KEY) are set
  // via `wrangler secret put <NAME>` or the dashboard — NOT here. Secrets persist
  // across deploys.
  //
  // FROM_EMAIL and SITE_URL are NOT secrets, so they live in `vars` below. This
  // is deliberate: a plaintext var set ONLY in the dashboard is WIPED on the next
  // `wrangler deploy` (the CI deploy treats this file as the source of truth for
  // vars), whereas secrets persist. Keeping them here means they survive deploys.
  //
  // SITE_URL is read at RUNTIME by siteUrl() (lib/url.ts) for verify/Stripe/
  // metadata links. It is intentionally NOT named NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL: public
  // vars are inlined at BUILD time, so a local `deploy:cf` build would bake in
  // whatever `.env.local` has (http://localhost:3000) — which is exactly how
  // reservation verify links ended up pointing at localhost. A plain runtime var
  // dodges that entirely.
  //
  // Public NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars are inlined at build time. They need to be in
  // the build environment, set in the Cloudflare dashboard under
  // Settings → Variables & Secrets before each deploy.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "doughbie-app",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-03-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],
  "vars": {
    "FROM_EMAIL": "Doughbie <hello@doughbiebakes.com>",
    "SITE_URL": "https://www.doughbiebakes.com"
  },
  "assets": {
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "directory": ".open-next/assets"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
